well you can see from this graph just  how quickly inflation has caught up with  wage growth at two-point-three percent  it's now at the same level as the latest  date of a pay growth which covers the  three months to January and that's  fueling concern of another cost of  living crisis or frances o'grady general  secretary the trade union congress is in  our westminster studio and sarah huyn is  the chief economist for europe a  standard chartered Sarah I'll start with  you first I mean how much of this  increase do you think is down to the  post referendum fall in the pant not  feeding into domestic price pressures  well not too much so far at the main  driver of higher inflation that was  reported today has been rising transport  costs down to higher fuel prices if we  look at how far all prices have risen  over the past year in sterling terms  they've pretty much doubled so that's  been the main driver we're starting to  see something of an impact from the  weaker pound feeding through into high  import costs but we'll probably see much  more of that to come in the coming  months what about the bank of england's  reaction to this i mean everyone's  assuming there's going to be no increase  in bank rate this year is that now back  on the table is that up for discussion  again it's certainly up for discussion  and we know that some policymakers are  feeling a little bit uneasy about  holding rates so low for such a long  time when you're facing a rise a sharp  rise in inflation but from the bank of  england's point of view the higher  inflation is a risk to the economy it  cups people spending capacity and that  could mean that growth slows and will in  which case you don't want to be raising  interest rates well frances o'grady what  would you make of what sir has just been  saying how worried are you about a rise  in Livingston squeeze on living  standards I think working families up  and down the country are worried about  that they're facing this double blow of  rising prices and no real wage increase  and of course the risk is that people go  back to building up household debt as  the only way to get by and we know what  mess that got us into last time so we  need some government action and a little  less complacency from government  ministers about what we see is  lubing living standard crisis so what  specifically then would you like to see  the government doing bearing in mind  that you know the deficit is still going  to be something like 51 billion pounds  this financial year and about the same  next year that we've got working  families all over the country facing a  real cut in their living standards and  the root of this problem is that we've  got too many insecure low-paid jobs and  too little investments that will deliver  the kind of high-skilled high paid jobs  that not just families need the country  needs Britain is still in the bottom  three of the OECD League for total  investment that needs to change we  wanted to see that change in the last  budget perhaps we'll get it in the next  one why do you think that pay  settlements look like the risk of  failing to keep up with inflation that I  think the one prescription that the  government hasn't tried is putting more  bargaining power in the hands of working  people we've seen this massive growth in  zero hours and bogus self-employment  where workers are too frightened to put  the head above the parapet and ask for a  pay rise we've seen attacks on trade  unions instead of encouraging trade  unions to grow to balance up that power  deficit a bit but also of course we've  had that pay cap or nurses firefighters  midwives a year after year they've  suffered real pay cuts and you know they  just can't carry on on that basis I  think we have a government that has  promised to serve the interests of  working people and now we need some  evidence of it so restore one way of  restoring the playing field between the  self-employed and the employ of course  was raising national insurance  contributions for the self-employed you  saying the Chancellor should have held  his nerve last week we I think we're  very clear that the real problem here is  a growth in self-employment that we  would describe as bogus where we've seen  too many employers taking workers off  employee contracts and putting them on  to self-employed contracts even though  they're controlling their work and  they're not genuinely self-employed we  would like to have seen the Chancellor  deal with that I think if you were  to reform the tax system to make it  fairer between the self employed in the  employees then you need to make sure  that the self-employed get the same  benefits and rights that employees get  and currently they don't get sick pay  they don't get holiday pay they pay for  their own training you know we would  need to see a pretty radical overhaul of  the rights the self-employed enjoy  before you hit them over the head with  tax increases so where does inflation go  from here what are your expectations for  the rest of the year we're expecting  inflation to carry on rising we think  that it will very quickly move above  three percent probably by the middle of  the year and then it's likely to stay  between two and three percent over the  next couple of years we'll continue to  see the impact of the weaker pound  feeding through to higher import prices  and that impact is going to linger on  and some people would say this is  actually a positive in some ways I mean  lord King the governor of the Bank of  England had said that a fall in the  pound the former governor he said it  fall in the pan was inevitable this was  all essential if the economy was going  to be rebalanced away from exports and  away from imports and towards exports it  certainly ought to help export exports  have become a lot more competitive as a  result and I think the expectations are  that exports will rise imports are more  expensive so that ought to slow the  growth in in imports it ought to help  our balance of payments the question is  what's happening in the rest of the  economy we've relied on consumer  spending to drive growth as Francis says  investment has been very weak recently  and if households feel that their their  incomes are squeezed either they have to  borrow more they have to draw on their  savings or they have to spend less I  think the bank of england's concern is  that households will spend less and that  could end up being a problem for the  economy worth pointing out so I mean you  are you are lest we forget European  chief economist at standard chartered I  mean we're starting to see inflation  tickle higher in in mainland Europe as  well only yes that's right and it's a  big question for the Bank of England and  for the European Central Bank both  central banks have interest rates very  low European Central Bank is due  in quantitative easing Bank of England  up until recently had a quantitative  easing package so for both banks the  question is when do you start to raise  interest rates and I think both central  banks are looking at what happens to  wages if wages accelerate that will be  the signal to start to raise interest  rates Frances well we have you here a  quick question on on unions more  generally at the moment Tom Watson  yesterday suggesting that momentum is  trying to infiltrate unite unite may  start to fund the momentum group how  would I about the consequences for the  Labour Party of that the TC isn't  affiliated to any party but what I am  care about is that we need an effective  opposition any democratic country needs  a good opposition holding the government  to account and my goodness me working  people out in the country really need a  strong opposition fighting for them  aren't making the case for good skill  jobs in the parts of Britain that need  the most is jeremy corbyn's Labour Party  not providing an effective opposition at  the moment then well I don't think it's  any secret that if a party is fighting  within itself then it's not doing the  job of opposition as well as many  working people need it to but let's be  clear that it's not just the opposition  that's got divisions I think we've seen  a fair few divisions in government too  not least over the manner of the brexit  that we have and whether this is going  to be a brexit that protects our economy  delivers good jobs protects people's  livelihoods or whether we're going to be  captured by these ultras on the back  benches in the Conservative Party who  seemed to not want any kind of good deal  with what is our most important trading  partner the EU

